	<key>readme</key>
	<string>Calculate with real units, physics constants, and measurement uncertainty, powered by [Qalculate](https://qalculate.github.io).

## Setup

Install Qalculate's command-line tool with [Homebrew](https://brew.sh):

```
brew install libqalculate
```

If it is missing, the workflow shows a result to copy the install command.

## Usage

Type the keyword (default: `calc`) followed by an expression.

* &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Copy the result.
* &lt;kbd&gt;⌘&lt;/kbd&gt;&lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Paste the result into the frontmost app.
* &lt;kbd&gt;⌥&lt;/kbd&gt;&lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Copy the full line (expression = result).

Examples:

```
calc 10 m / 2 s                                  → 5 m/s
calc 5 N * 2 m to J                              → 10 J
calc 60 mph to m/s                               → 26.8224 m/s
calc planck * c / (500 nm) to eV                 → 2.479683969 eV
calc (5.0+/-0.1) m / (2.00+/-0.05) s             → 2.500±0.080 m/s
calc G * 5.972e24 kg / (6.371e6 m)^2             → 9.820 m/s²
calc solve(x^2 + 2x = 8)                         → [2, -4]
```

Units simplify automatically (N·m → J, V/Ω → A). Convert with `to`, e.g. `0.0063 m to mm`.

Constants by name: `c`, `G`, `planck`, `hbar`, `k_B`, `N_A`, `q_e`, `m_e`, `g0`, `epsilon_0`, `mu_0`. A bare `e` is Euler's number.

Write uncertainty as `(value+/-error)` and it propagates through the calculation.

Trigonometry uses degrees (`sin(90) = 1`); add `rad` for radians.

## Configuration

Set the keyword in the [Workflow Configuration](https://www.alfredapp.com/help/workflows/user-configuration/). Results ignore your personal `qalc.cfg`, so they are consistent.
</string>
